-- title:
--   Normality of the crossing ring W[x,y]/(xy-t)
-- statement:
--   Let $W$ be a commutative ring which is an integral domain with unique factorisation, and let $t \in W$ be a nonzero element. Form the quotient [`MvPolynomial.CrossingQuotient W t`](def/MvPolynomial_CrossingQuotient.html#L11), namely the polynomial ring $W[X_0,X_1]$ in two variables indexed by `Fin 2` modulo the principal ideal generated by $X_0X_1 - t$; that is, the affine coordinate ring $W[x,y]/(xy-t)$ of the plane crossing $xy = t$ over $W$. The theorem asserts the conjunction of two statements about this ring: first, that it is an integral domain (nontrivial, with no zero divisors), and second, that it satisfies Mathlib's `IsIntegrallyClosed`, i.e. every element of its field of fractions that is integral over it already lies in its image, so that the ring is integrally closed in its fraction field. Together the two conjuncts say that $W[x,y]/(xy-t)$ is a normal domain for every nonzero $t$, with no irreducibility or squarefreeness hypothesis on $t$.
--
--   This is the standard local model of an ordinary double point: for $W$ a discrete valuation ring with uniformiser $\varpi$ and $t = \varpi^e$ one obtains $\mathcal{O}[u,v]/(uv-\varpi^e)$, normal for all $e \ge 1$ though regular only for $e = 1$. It is used in the Deligne–Rapoport model of a modular curve, supplying normality of stalks and the existence of oriented crossing charts.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_MvPolynomial_CrossingQuotient_isDomain_and_isIntegrallyClosed.lean

